      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let's denote the cost of one pen by \\( p \\) and the cost of one pencil by \\( c \\). The problem gives us two pieces of information:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost \\$2.24.\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost \\$1.54.\n\nWe can translate these statements into the following equations:\n\\[ 4p + 3c = 224 \\quad \\text{(1)} \\]\n\\[ 2p + 5c = 154 \\quad \\text{(2)} \\]\n\nFirst, we will eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations the same. We can do this by multiplying equation (2) by 2:\n\\[ 4p + 10c = 308 \\quad \\text{(3)} \\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[ 4p + 3c = 224 \\quad \\text{(1)} \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10c = 308 \\quad \\text{(3)} \\]\n\nNext, we subtract equation (1) from equation (3):\n\\[ (4p + 10c) - (4p + 3c) = 308 - 224 \\]\n\\[ 7c = 84 \\]\n\\[ c = \\frac{84}{7} \\]\n\\[ c = 12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of one pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. To find the cost of one pen, we substitute \\( c = 12 \\) back into equation (2):\n\\[ 2p + 5(12) = 154 \\]\n\\[ 2p + 60 = 154 \\]\n\\[ 2p = 154 - 60 \\]\n\\[ 2p = 94 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{94}{2} \\]\n\\[ p = 47 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of one pen is \\( 47 \\) cents. The final answer for the cost of a pencil is:\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]",
