      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the cost of a pencil, we need to set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let's denote the cost of a pen by \\( p \\) cents and the cost of a pencil by \\( c \\) cents.\n\nFrom the problem, we have the following two equations:\n1. \\( 4p + 3c = 224 \\) (since \\( 2.24 = 224 \\) cents)\n2. \\( 2p + 5c = 154 \\) (since \\( 1.54 = 154 \\) cents)\n\nWe will solve this system of equations step by step.\n\nFirst, let's label the equations for convenience:\n\\[ 4p + 3c = 224 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)} \\]\n\\[ 2p + 5c = 154 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)} \\]\n\nTo eliminate \\( p \\), we can multiply Equation 2 by 2:\n\\[ 4p + 10c = 308 \\quad \\text{(Equation 3)} \\]\n\nNow, we subtract Equation 1 from Equation 3:\n\\[ (4p + 10c) - (4p + 3c) = 308 - 224 \\]\n\\[ 7c = 84 \\]\n\\[ c = \\frac{84}{7} \\]\n\\[ c = 12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. To verify, we can substitute \\( c = 12 \\) back into Equation 2 to find \\( p \\):\n\\[ 2p + 5(12) = 154 \\]\n\\[ 2p + 60 = 154 \\]\n\\[ 2p = 94 \\]\n\\[ p = 47 \\]\n\nNow, we check if these values satisfy Equation 1:\n\\[ 4p + 3c = 4(47) + 3(12) = 188 + 36 = 224 \\]\n\nSince the values satisfy both equations, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
